(project tuna-stdlib
 (doc "Tuna book chapter: the stdlib for the surface dialect. The
      language's provisional name is sabra - the v0-handoff runner-up;
      the tuna-the-system vs sabra-the-language split is the owner's
      call, nothing in this chapter depends on it, and every artifact
      is named stdlib regardless. This is the vocabulary layer the
      2026-10-02 design review gated the roadmap on: substrate dogfood
      already happened (host-written products - todo, view, trace,
      rewind); in-calculus product logic is NOT dogfooded until this
      chapter lands, else the language reads as unusable when it is
      merely bare. Research question, stated so it can be refuted:
      package-scale writability is reachable through DICTIONARY
      DEFINITIONS ALONE - zero new surface syntax, zero
      interpreter/, compiler/, common/ changes; the host gains
      seeding and one resolution probe, nothing else. That is the
      Forth endgame bet made falsifiable.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section stdlib-v1
  (doc "A stdlib def is an ordinary def: compile + program upsert
        (IR + provenance retained) + a name->tree dictionary row +
        one journaled first-class run. No new linking semantics are
        introduced: names resolve at parse time (capture-safe; a
        shadowing lambda parameter wins), dictionary-bound names read
        as tree literals, and cross-def references inline the
        referenced compiled trees at def time. The stdlib is a seeded
        dictionary owned by a dedicated identity - plus probe order.
        Conventions are INTENSIONAL per the vendored upstream
        conventions README: booleans, options, lists, naturals as
        tree structure directly, because the triage rules give
        structural dispatch free where Church-style extensional
        encodings pay for it at every use. Extensional stays where it
        already lives: differential corpus fixtures (add_ch_2_3,
        church3_succ_zero).")
  (status planned)
  (subsection conventions
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Life on an untyped substrate: conventions COLLIDE and that is
      recorded, not prevented. false = nil = none = leaf; some leaf =
      true; pair (false, false) *is* the 2-list and also the canonical
      int zero the moment anything reads it as one. Predicates and
      canonical forms are the tools, laws below say which predicate
      reads which convention.

      1. BOOL. false = 0, true = %10 (leaf; stem leaf) - upstream's
         two smallest trees, distinguished by a single triage
         reduction. The cstr byte codec already bakes the same
         assignment in (byte-values chapter), so ONE encoding of bool
         exists in the system, not two.
      2. LIST. nil = leaf, cons = fork hd tl - upstream's algebraic
         list. cstr strings already ride the identical convention
         (string = list of chars, char = little-endian bit list of
         bools): one list law, never a second one.
      3. OPTION. none = leaf, some x = stem x (upstream). Collides
         with bools by design; option answers are case-read, never
         boolean-tested.
      4. SMALL NAT. stem chains (0 = leaf, n+1 = stem n) for tags
         and counters only. Constructors cost zero triage: succ IS
         the apply-Leaf wrapper, pair IS Leaf - the byte-values law
         4 cousin; costs live in consumers, never constructors.
      5. INT. sign-magnitude: fork of bool sign and a magnitude bit
         list, least-significant first (aligned with the cstr char
         bit order), high-order false bits stripped by
         int-canonical; both zeros one form (fork false nil).
         Decimal/currency conventions are NOT here - a future ledger
         chapter owns amounts-as-fixed-point-cents, so the pure
         numeric core stays a pure integer core.
      6. DECIDED OUT OF V1 (not gaps; adding one is a book change
         citing measured need): record/assoc conventions; string
         payload ops (byte-values + prim world already - pure string
         ops are priced like unary bloat per byte-values law 4);
         journaled math prims (see the numeric-fuel failure below);
         surface sugar - letrec, list or number literals - belongs
         to a DIALECT chapter, not here. OPERATOR HUNCH PINNED
         2026-10-02, naming that chapter's first moves in advance:
         BRACKET FORMS AS CONSTRUCTION ([a b c] desugars at the
         reader to cons-applications the compiler already accepts
         - zero IR change, zero core change, structural
         construction contributes zero triage: data forms that
         cannot evaluate, parens stay computation - the grammar
         shows the cost model law 4 already states) plus NUMBER
         LITERALS decoding to int law 5. nopales-side pin, to be
         recorded in the PP book: brackets FORBIDDEN there, so the
         paren-habitat / bracket-calculus tell survives the F3
         bridge. Identity is the soft driver; the MEASURED need
         is fixture/corpus authoring (mole postings, forensics
         faults) where hand-ternary is hostile. Syntax stays
         frozen until this stdlib's make lands - Forth law
         intact. |))))
  (subsection core-v1
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      Layered so every entry can name what it consumes; every def
      written in TODAY's dialect (lambda, application, %ternary pins,
      strings, pair/cons).

      L0 PINS (verbatim-port rule, AGENTS 3): not = %22102000,
      size 8, ported as-is - acceptance M3 pins not true -> leaf
      in 2 steps (rules 3b then 3a) and the corpus already carries
      id_not. true and false as named alias defs so docs, agents and
      tests can cite them.

      L1 STRUCTURE: tree-case - the three-arm typecase over
      leaf/stem/fork, branch functions receiving the parts (stem
      takes (child), fork takes (l r), leaf takes the branch value
      plain). THE pattern-calculus showpiece and the single load-
      bearing bridge into the lambda surface: everything structural
      is defined through it. No published upstream tree is vendored
      for the typecase (reference READMEs checked), so this def is
      tuna-derived and PROPERTY-PINNED: a result+steps table over
      sample leaf/stem/fork inputs lands in this chapter at make
      time, and a published upstream version supersedes ours per the
      verbatim-port rule. leaf?/stem?/fork? predicates join it, and
      the bool core: if (bool then/else, landing on the upstream
      single-triage dispatch).

      L2 LISTS: list-fold FIRST - the recursion idiom; consumers
      are defined via fold wherever expressible so the stdlib keeps
      ONE recursion shape instead of five (fewer distinct closures
      means less bracket-abstraction blowup and a sharing-v1
      friendlier graph). Then list-length, list-reverse,
      list-append, list-map, list-ref (option answer per law 3),
      list-eq, and tree-eq - generic structural equality over
      arbitrary trees including programs, itself a property table
      class (T-family style) at make time.

      L3 INTS: int-canonical, int-zero, int-one, int-neg, int-add,
      int-sub (add + neg), int-cmp, int-mul - carry-propagating
      folds over the magnitude bit lists; every op returns canonical
      form (law 5), so no consumer can accumulate representation
      debt. mul priced honestly: it is the first entry where fuel
      cost is expected to be interesting - see the FUEL TABLE law. |))))
  (subsection seeding
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      SOURCE: stdlib/v1/core.defs - def-commands in the existing
      REPL grammar, one per record, blank-line separated; file
      order = compile order = topological order (pins before
      tree-case before consumers). The file IS a replayable REPL
      transcript; no second grammar ever.

      OWNER: a dedicated non-admin identity sabralib (booted like
      fed peers; token once, rotation via env). Seeding replays each
      def through the ordinary def round - so every stdlib entry
      lands as a program row (hash-citable, IR + provenance
      attached), a repl_dict row, and a journaled run attributed to
      sabralib, parent-chained in file order. The stdlib's own
      installation is therefore auditable and deriv-able like
      everything else - no privileged write path.

      RESOLUTION ORDER (parse time; the ONLY new host behavior):
      lambda param > identity repl_dict > sabralib repl_dict >
      reader builtins. The server assembles the effective
      dictionary as std-under-identity - one name->tree map, the
      compiler untouched. Consequence, adopted from PP's boot law:
      seeding is ADDITIVE, skip-if-exists, never overwrite; an
      identity that pins its own not shadows std locally, undef
      restores it. Explicit reseed (admin) touches only the
      sabralib dictionary - PP's reseed-package escape hatch,
      carried over.

      RELEASE MANIFEST: stdlib/v1/manifest pins name -> ternary
      size -> program hash for every def; the verify script
      recomputes from the store and diffs. A release whose content
      changes lands as new hashes and fresh rows - the manifest
      bumps, history stays content-addressed, nothing mutates. |))))
  (subsection acceptance
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      1. MANIFEST DRIFT. The implement-size table lands in this
         chapter (every def: name, size, program hash);
         scripts/verify-8-stdlib.sh recomputes from the live store
         and diffs - exit 1 on any drift.
      2. DIFFERENTIAL. Every def is exercised by at least one new
         corpus entry; the 26-entry corpus grows; three engines
         agree result + step count (v0 law; v1 distinct counts
         recorded alongside per sharing.borg, never equality).
      3. FUEL TABLE (load-bearing, recorded here at make time):
         measured v0 steps for tree-case arm dispatch, list-fold
         over 10/100-element lists, int-add over 8/32/64-bit
         operands, int-mul over 8/16-bit; v1 sharing numbers beside
         them. This table feeds both pre-registered failures.
      4. SEED DISCIPLINE. Double boot: identical rows, zero
         duplicates, every def journaled and attributed. Local-pin
         shadow test: pinning a std name in a user identity wins;
         undef reveals std. Reseed is explicit-only and touches
         only sabralib's dictionary.
      5. LEDGER BRIDGE. One mole-scenario run: a posting list folded
         to a balance by the int core, status normal, replay-
         verified, deriv-sealed and deriv-check exit 0 - the first
         product candidate the 2026-10-02 review named, proving the
         stdlib carries a validator-shaped workload end to end.
      6. CORE UNTOUCHED. interpreter/, compiler/, common/ stay
         byte-identical in the landing commit; host delta = seeding
         + probe order only. If any stdlib def requires a core
         change to work, that is a FINDING, not a patch. |))))
  (subsection pre-registered-failures
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
      F5 INLINE BLOWUP. Def-time inlining multiplies compiled size
      through def chains (the body of B lands inside every A that
      consumes it). TRIGGER: any v1 stdlib def compiles above 100k
      nodes, or a consumer layer exceeds 10x the size of the layer
      it consumes. THEN: the dictionary-as-module model is
      insufficient as-is - record in FINDINGS.md and take a linking
      chapter; do NOT rescue by growing kernel vocabulary here.

      F6 NUMERIC FUEL - the purity flip, pre-decided 2026-10-02
      review (pure-checks / prim-aggregates). TRIGGER: 64-bit
      int-add exceeds 100k steps under v0, or trips the compile cap
      on seeding. THEN: a follow-up chapter adds a journaled math
      prim family (math/add, math/cmp, ...) and default arithmetic
      moves to prims - while INVARIANT checks (ledger balance
      rules) stay pure, so replay recomputes the checks that
      matter and trusts recorded answers exactly where the review
      drew that line. Fuel table law 3 carries both engines'
      numbers into the finding. |))))))
